text:
RPC Node Manipulation and Eclipse via Malicious RPC

Description:
Attackers can run malicious RPC nodes that serve false or stale data to
clients, manipulating application behavior without compromising the blockchain.

Attack patterns:
1. Serve stale account state (old balances, wrong data)
2. Return false transaction confirmations
3. Withhold transaction submission (censor transactions)
4. Return manipulated getRecentBlockhash causing transaction failures
5. Man-in-the-middle between client and real validators

Vulnerable client pattern:
```javascript
// Trusting single RPC endpoint
const connection = new Connection('https://malicious-rpc.com');
const balance = await connection.getBalance(wallet);
// balance could be manipulated!
```

Secure pattern:
```javascript
// Multi-RPC verification
const rpcs = [Connection1, Connection2, Connection3];
const balances = await Promise.all(rpcs.map(r => r.getBalance(wallet)));
const consensus = balances.filter(b => b === balances[0]).length > balances.length/2;
require(consensus, 'RPC disagreement detected');

// Use commitment levels
const balance = await connection.getBalance(wallet, 'finalized');
```

Detection:
- Response inconsistency across multiple RPC endpoints
- Latency anomalies vs known good endpoints
- Commitment level mismatches

Mitigation:
- Use multiple RPC providers (Helius + Triton + QuickNode)
- Always use 'finalized' commitment for critical operations
- Verify critical state on-chain directly
Source: Helius RPC best practices, Solana security guide
